WEBVTT

00:04.650 --> 00:06.963
In the last lesson, you saw what can happen when

00:06.963 --> 00:09.750
a miner can't speak up. This lesson is about how

00:09.750 --> 00:12.425
to speak up, and what protects you when you do.

00:13.548 --> 00:16.210
Under the Mine Act, "miner" is broad.

00:16.210 --> 00:19.061
It means generally anyone working at a mine:

00:19.061 --> 00:22.335
employees, contractors, construction workers,

00:22.335 --> 00:24.598
and truck drivers who are regularly exposed to

00:24.598 --> 00:27.686
mine hazards. Supervisors who work in the mine

00:27.686 --> 00:30.436
are miners too. So all these rights are yours from

00:30.436 --> 00:31.548
your first day.

00:34.870 --> 00:37.733
In almost every case, the fastest way to fix a

00:37.733 --> 00:40.483
hazard is to tell somebody who can fix it.

00:40.483 --> 00:43.383
At Allen, any hazard can be reported verbally to

00:43.383 --> 00:46.458
your supervisor. Equipment problems also go on

00:46.458 --> 00:49.883
your pre-trip inspection list or app. Supervisors

00:49.883 --> 00:52.608
record hazards on daily inspection sheets,

00:52.608 --> 00:55.645
and you can log an observation in the HCSS app,

00:55.645 --> 00:58.195
which goes to upper management. The line at every

00:58.195 --> 01:00.095
site runs up to Hunter Kenney.

01:01.216 --> 01:03.879
MSHA's guide also says that when you refuse to

01:03.879 --> 01:07.178
work in conditions you reasonably believe are unsafe,

01:07.178 --> 01:09.728
you have a responsibility to tell the operator,

01:09.728 --> 01:11.416
so they have a chance to fix it.

01:12.538 --> 01:15.475
But you never need permission to contact MSHA.

01:15.475 --> 01:19.138
You can call, write, or use MSHA's online form

01:19.138 --> 01:22.213
at any time, and the law protects you from retaliation

01:22.213 --> 01:23.188
for doing it.

01:24.316 --> 01:26.466
If you ever believe a job is unsafe,

01:26.466 --> 01:29.354
don't just walk off. Say what you're worried about,

01:29.354 --> 01:32.216
and why, to your lead or supervisor.

01:32.216 --> 01:34.504
Don't do the unsafe part while it's being sorted

01:34.504 --> 01:37.116
out. That keeps you protected, and it gives the

01:37.116 --> 01:40.191
company a chance to fix it. Module 12 covers how

01:40.191 --> 01:41.966
to stop work in detail.

01:45.278 --> 01:48.716
MSHA runs a hazard hotline. It's one eight hundred,

01:48.716 --> 01:51.516
seven four six, fifteen fifty-three,

01:51.516 --> 01:54.341
and it's answered every hour of every day.

01:54.341 --> 01:56.516
You do not have to identify yourself.

01:56.516 --> 01:59.728
You can also report a hazard on MSHA's website,

01:59.728 --> 02:01.028
msha.gov.

02:02.144 --> 02:04.969
If you can, give MSHA the company name,

02:04.969 --> 02:07.407
the name of the mine, the town and state,

02:07.407 --> 02:10.094
and the MSHA ID number, if you know it.

02:10.094 --> 02:12.907
For us, that's Boonesboro, fifteen,

02:12.907 --> 02:16.532
zero zero zero zero six. Clover Bottom number one,

02:16.532 --> 02:20.431
fifteen, zero zero one one two, and number two,

02:20.431 --> 02:23.257
fifteen, one nine eight nine eight.

02:23.257 --> 02:27.007
And Dix River, fifteen, one eight three eight nine.

02:28.130 --> 02:30.667
MSHA says that if a complaint describes where the

02:30.667 --> 02:34.118
hazard is, and what it is, MSHA will inspect as

02:34.118 --> 02:36.630
soon as possible. Complaints made this way don't

02:36.630 --> 02:38.555
have to be shared with the operator,

02:38.555 --> 02:40.905
and the operator may not be told a complaint was

02:40.905 --> 02:41.630
made.

02:44.976 --> 02:47.526
There is also a formal route, called a Section

02:47.526 --> 02:51.689
103(g) request for inspection. It's for when you

02:51.689 --> 02:53.814
believe there's an imminent danger,

02:53.814 --> 02:57.114
a violation of the Act, or a violation of a mandatory

02:57.114 --> 03:00.289
safety or health standard. It must be in writing

03:00.289 --> 03:03.364
and signed, by you or by your representative.

03:03.364 --> 03:06.976
In exchange, MSHA keeps your name confidential.

03:06.976 --> 03:09.126
It does not give the operator your name,

03:09.126 --> 03:11.464
or anything that would reveal who you are,

03:11.464 --> 03:14.064
like a specific work area or shift that would point

03:14.064 --> 03:14.776
to you.

03:15.894 --> 03:18.381
A formal request has another benefit.

03:18.381 --> 03:22.331
If MSHA decides not to inspect, or finds nothing,

03:22.331 --> 03:24.731
you get a written notice from the District Manager,

03:24.731 --> 03:27.394
and you can appeal in writing within ten days.

03:27.394 --> 03:29.331
If you're unsure which route to use,

03:29.331 --> 03:32.444
MSHA can help you decide. Call the hotline.

03:33.576 --> 03:35.688
You also have the right to have a representative

03:35.688 --> 03:39.313
go along with an MSHA inspector. A miners' representative

03:39.313 --> 03:42.351
is someone chosen by two or more miners to speak

03:42.351 --> 03:44.651
for them on safety and health, and they do not

03:44.651 --> 03:46.963
have to work at the mine. Right now,

03:46.963 --> 03:50.226
none of our sites has a designated miners' representative.

03:50.226 --> 03:52.801
If no one is there when an inspector arrives,

03:52.801 --> 03:54.951
the inspector talks with a reasonable number of

03:54.951 --> 03:58.201
miners instead. If MSHA ever talks to you,

03:58.201 --> 04:00.788
tell the truth. That's your responsibility,

04:00.788 --> 04:02.751
and you're protected for taking part.

04:06.094 --> 04:10.869
Section 105(c) of the Act protects you from retaliation

04:10.869 --> 04:13.557
when you use your rights. That includes reporting

04:13.557 --> 04:17.669
a danger or a violation to MSHA, to the operator,

04:17.669 --> 04:20.370
or to a miners' representative. It includes taking

04:20.370 --> 04:23.257
part in an inspection or an investigation.

04:23.257 --> 04:25.732
It includes withdrawing from the mine if you haven't

04:25.732 --> 04:28.507
had the required training. And it includes refusing

04:28.507 --> 04:31.195
to work in conditions you reasonably believe are

04:31.195 --> 04:33.144
unsafe or unhealthy.

04:34.276 --> 04:36.788
Retaliation isn't just being fired.

04:36.788 --> 04:40.339
MSHA lists demotion, lower pay or hours,

04:40.339 --> 04:43.251
harassment, threats, and a transfer to a worse

04:43.251 --> 04:45.088
job, among others.

04:46.222 --> 04:48.122
If you believe you've been punished for using your

04:48.122 --> 04:51.247
rights, here's what to do. First, write down what

04:51.247 --> 04:54.360
happened, with dates, names, places,

04:54.360 --> 04:57.760
and times. Second, keep copies of anything you

04:57.760 --> 05:01.897
receive or send, including emails and text messages.

05:01.897 --> 05:06.135
Third, contact MSHA within sixty days of the event.

05:06.135 --> 05:08.322
You may have extra time in special cases,

05:08.322 --> 05:12.272
but don't count on it. Fourth, give MSHA the details.

05:12.272 --> 05:15.535
Fifth, MSHA investigates. And sixth,

05:15.535 --> 05:17.872
MSHA tells you its decision in writing,

05:17.872 --> 05:19.334
within ninety days.

05:20.464 --> 05:24.127
If MSHA finds discrimination, Department of Labor

05:24.127 --> 05:26.927
attorneys can ask the Review Commission for relief,

05:26.927 --> 05:29.439
such as getting your job back and back pay.

05:29.439 --> 05:32.639
If MSHA finds no violation, you can still take

05:32.639 --> 05:34.539
your case to the Commission yourself,

05:34.539 --> 05:35.989
within thirty days.

05:37.114 --> 05:40.364
MSHA gives some practical advice. Keep a calendar

05:40.364 --> 05:43.139
and good records. Hold important conversations

05:43.139 --> 05:45.502
with management with other people present.

05:45.502 --> 05:48.139
Don't take documents that belong to your employer,

05:48.139 --> 05:51.151
like required books and records. And don't break

05:51.151 --> 05:53.627
any law while you're exercising your rights.

05:53.627 --> 05:55.739
If you need help, call MSHA.

05:56.873 --> 06:00.322
One more right is worth knowing. If MSHA issues

06:00.322 --> 06:02.798
a withdrawal order and you're idled by it,

06:02.798 --> 06:04.947
you're entitled to your regular pay for the rest

06:04.947 --> 06:07.535
of that shift. If the order isn't lifted by the

06:07.535 --> 06:10.347
next shift, miners on that shift are paid for the

06:10.347 --> 06:13.060
time they're idled, up to four hours.

06:13.060 --> 06:15.373
If the operator doesn't comply with a standard

06:15.373 --> 06:17.135
and you're withdrawn because of it,

06:17.135 --> 06:20.197
there is pay for lost time too, up to a limit set

06:20.197 --> 06:22.923
in the Act. If you think you're owed pay and haven't

06:22.923 --> 06:26.860
received it, contact MSHA, because deadlines apply.

06:30.195 --> 06:33.032
To sum up. Start by telling your supervisor.

06:33.032 --> 06:35.495
You never need permission to call MSHA,

06:35.495 --> 06:37.982
and the hotline takes your call without your name.

06:37.982 --> 06:41.144
For a formal inspection request, write and sign

06:41.144 --> 06:45.169
a 103(g) request. If you're punished for using

06:45.169 --> 06:47.857
your rights, write it down, keep copies,

06:47.857 --> 06:50.820
and contact MSHA within sixty days.

06:50.820 --> 06:53.157
These rights are yours from the day you start.

06:54.272 --> 06:57.247
Your site's plans always override this video.
